=========================== Release 0.7.1 ==============================

2026-10-20  Yury Kirsanov
        * [upstream] Read-through and write-through in shard and spread:
          a through collection in shard reads and writes a key at its
          owner, in spread at its K holders; the drain sweep takes each
          node's own share; the fleet table shows each member's copies.

        * [upstream] A client reads its own write-through: inside
          upstream_echo_ms of a node's own write, a fetch of the key goes
          to the master, not a replica that may not have the write yet
          (before, a GET right after a SET could answer nil or the value
          before it).  The echo of a node's own write refetches the key
          instead of leaving it uncached (echo_refetches,
          echo_master_fetches).

        * [upstream] A fetch overtaken by an invalidation of its key is
          fetched again: the reply of a fetch that read the value before
          a change could land after the change's invalidation and was
          kept - the older value, until the key changed again.  Read-
          through in general (inflight_invalidated, inflight_refetches).

        * [upstream] After a tracking gap - a restart, a lost tracking
          connection - the copies held are fetched again in the
          background, newest written first (upstream_rewarm_max), so a
          node does not come back cold.

        * [upstream] Writes to one key from many clients at once, written
          through, leave the node holding what Redis holds: the read-backs
          are applied in the order Redis applied the writes, and an older
          one that arrives late is skipped (stale_readbacks_skipped).

        * [cluster] A member back after a silence raises
          cluster.peer_absent only when the retained deletes did not
          cover the time it was away, and the message names the limit
          that did not: tombstone_retain_s, or tombstone_retain_max
          dropping deletes made while it was away.  Before, any early
          drop by the cap raised it, worded as past tombstone_retain_s.

        * [page] A node holding a setting the fleet must agree on
          differently blinks its card, as an older build does.

        * [config] systemctl reload, SIGHUP or the privileged reload verb
          read the config again and apply the allow-lists, the secrets,
          log_level and slowlog_usec; any other changed key refuses the
          reload whole, naming the keys, and nothing changes.  Before,
          SIGHUP stopped the daemon.

        * [resp] The RESP door's DB-index map changes on a running
          fleet: perfcli map <index> <collection>, unmap <index> and
          maps, and the same from the page under the dashboard tool.
          Every connection sees the change on its next command, every
          member takes it, it survives a restart and outranks
          resp_collections until it is unmapped; stats and the page
          show each collection's DB index.  Refused while a member runs
          an older build.

        * [stats] Hot keys, always on: each node samples one request in
          64 and names the keys that pin a thread (stats, the page).

        * [dashboard] The fleet table describes the nodes (records,
          Redis copies), its fleet row counts each collection by its
          mode's rule, and each collection opens its members; in store
          mode the fleet's distinct keys are estimated from the members'
          sketches (the sketch verb).  A node asked for is that node or
          an error naming it - never another member's figures or
          actions.  The fleet mode is a badge beside the headings.

        * [upgrade] Each node speaks a range of wire epochs and the fleet
          commits the one it uses; an installed release records the
          range it speaks (share/perfcached/RELEASE), which the roll
          reads before it starts.

        * [docs] GUIDE: nginx in front: caching responses, with no
          module of our own.

        * [tests] The status page's script runs in CI (node), also as
          the dashboard draws it.

=========================== Release 0.7.0 ==============================

2026-10-16  Yury Kirsanov
        * [packaging] The daemon packages do not restart the daemon on
          upgrade: a clustered cache is restarted members first and the
          master last, one at a time; the postinst says so and
          PRODUCTION.md section 8 has the order and the pinning.  The
          dashboard package keeps the automatic restart.

        * [dashboard] The collections table does the live DDL: + new
          collection, and rename, resize and drop on each row (the
          confirmation names the entry count) - the daemon's own verbs
          on the selected node, announced to the fleet; the tool
          journals each.  A collection's settings stay configuration.

        * [dashboard] A build staged from the page: choose the tarball,
          its version and signature, and the selected member verifies
          and keeps it as upgrade_stage does (stage_max_mb, 64, bounds
          the upload).

        * [dashboard] The drain sweep from the page: drain and stop drain
          on the read-through line, which shows the sweep's state and
          figures (keys read, taken over, the fleet had, gone, failed).
          The daemon's updrain writes the audit line upfill does.

        * [dashboard] The fleet in one table under the member tiles:
          each member's version, install mode, staged builds and every
          collection's entry count side by side (the tool's /fleet).

        * [docs] One upgrade story: the support window (any patch of the
          same minor and the next minor roll; older ones step through each
          minor's last patch), the roll order, packages and tarball
          prefixes - the same in README, GUIDE and PRODUCTION, which
          had contradicted each other.

        * [alpine] Full Alpine packages: perfcached, perfcached-standalone,
          perfcached-dashboard, libperfd, libperfd-dev and perfcached-doc
          from one APKBUILD, with OpenRC services and the accounts.

        * [image] The admin image: perfcached-dashboard, perfcli and
          nginx in front, from contrib/docker/admin (podman or docker;
          tools/image-admin.sh builds and proves it; CI keeps the tar).
          compose.yml beside it runs two replicas behind one nginx (TLS
          and HTTP/2 there, a failed or 503 answer retried on the other
          replica); PCD_FRONT_PROXY passes the browser's address
          through for the lockout.
          Configured by a mounted file or PCD_SEEDS / PCD_SECRET[_FILE]
          / PCD_SESSION_KEY; TLS and HTTP/2 from mounted PEMs on 8443.

        * [dashboard] TLS in the tool itself: tls_cert and tls_key (PEM)
          serve https directly where no proxy fronts the dashboard;
          perfcached-dashboard -V says whether its libmicrohttpd has
          TLS.

        * [ci] The fleet suites and the roll under load also run on the
          epoll backend (PERFCACHED_URING_DENY=1), the backend a host
          without io_uring - or one configured io = epoll - runs.

        * [ci] The roll under load: a tag job rolls a three-member fleet
          with perfcli upgrade while a libperfd client reads and writes
          through it, and asserts no error, no lost key and no
          resurrection on every node (test/upgradeloadtest.sh,
          test/rollload.c, make check-roll) - in eager, and in shard with
          the WAL on, where the owner's share is away while it restarts.

        * [cli] perfcli upgrade plan|start|resume|status|abort|rollback:
          the roll driver (DESIGN 12ns) - stages a build on every
          member, then switches one node at a time, members first and
          the master last, with the gates between (restart-safe, a new
          pid on the version asked, ready with peers up, the peers'
          records kept); the first failure reverts that node and stops
          with the rollback commands printed; a journal records it and
          resume or rollback read it back.  A switch hook stands in for
          the root helper where there is none (the CI roll).

        * [cli] perfcli fleet-status: this node and every member, each
          dialled - version, state, install mode, the builds staged
          there - the upgrade leader's view in one table (UP8's first
          piece).

        * [lib] perfd_upgrade_stage / perfd_upgrade_stage_file: the
          staging of a release artifact (begin, 192 KB chunks, end, the
          signature, a progress callback) in the library; perfcli's
          upgrade-stage is this call.

        * [lib] libperfd 0.7.0: the operations verbs, typed -
          collections, create, drop, resize, rename, reset_stats, save,
          sync, load, mailtest, upgrade_status, fleetstop - beside 0.6.6's
          stats, members, clients, enable and the sweeps.  Every request
          perfcli and the dashboard send has a typed form now.

        * [http] The daemon serves no page of its own any more: GET /
          answers 410 Gone and names perfcached-dashboard, and the
          door is read-only (POST /reset-stats answers 405: the reset
          is the native verb, perfcli reset-stats or the dashboard's
          button); /stats, /members, /clients, /metrics and /health
          stay.  A dashboard under attack now costs a daemon nothing.

        * [native] name <name>: a connection names itself (CLIENT
          SETNAME's native form) - shown by clients, and the node's
          enable, upfill, updrain and reset-stats log lines say who
          (name@ip:port).  libperfd sends opts.name after every dial;
          perfcli is perfcli@<host> unless -N, the dashboard
          dashboard@<host>.

        * [native] upgrade_revert: the helper switches this node back to
          the prefix it kept before its last switch; upgrade_status
          reports previous (that switch), live_prefix and switching (a
          request the helper has taken).  perfcli upgrade-revert, and
          perfd_upgrade_revert in the library.

        * [native] upgrade_stage refuses a package install ("upgrade it
          with the package manager"); upgrade_status reports install_mode
          - package, prefix or local, detected (the packages write
          /usr/share/perfcached/INSTALL-MODE; a binary under
          /opt/perfcached-* is a prefix install) or set with [daemon]
          install_mode.

=========================== Release 0.6.6 ==============================

2026-10-15  Yury Kirsanov
        * [lib] libperfd 0.6.6: the admin verbs, typed - perfd_stats,
          perfd_members and perfd_clients answer the trees the status
          page reads, as JSON text; perfd_enable raises privilege and the
          handle raises it again by itself after a re-dial; perfd_upfill
          and perfd_updrain run, check or stop a sweep.  perfcli and the
          dashboard build no request by hand for them.

        * [native] clients {cap} - this node's connections as /clients
          renders them, on the native door.

        * [page] During a rolling upgrade the cards of the members still
          on the older build blink amber and say which build they run
          and which is the fleet's newest; the header's "mixed
          versions" alone did not point at the nodes left to do.

        * [dashboard] perfcached-dashboard: the status page served by its
          own process, a client of the fleet's native door through
          libperfd (one connection per member, encrypted end to end), so
          a dashboard under attack stalls no daemon.  The page is the
          daemon's, byte for byte, under the same script-hash CSP; the
          node switch moves between members; the write-through line gets
          check, upfill and stop, and reset stats stays - each asks the
          fleet's enable secret once per browser page.  Its HTTP layer is
          libmicrohttpd (HTTP/1.1, dual-stack; TLS and HTTP/2 belong to a
          reverse proxy in front); a POST needs a custom request header,
          a JSON body under body_max and the session token; wrong secrets
          lock the address out.  Shipped as its own package with its own
          user, a disabled unit and a resource cap; built where
          libmicrohttpd-dev is installed (PC_DASH=auto).  The daemon's
          own page stays, deprecated, and goes in 0.7.0.  Several
          dashboards: instances sharing a session_key honour each
          other's session tokens (the token carries the session,
          encrypted; the servers hold nothing), /health answers 503 when
          no member is reachable, and the unit is not ordered on the
          daemon - a dashboard runs on any machine.  contrib/ carries a
          HAProxy and an nginx front end for N dashboards; trusted_proxy
          names the proxies whose X-Forwarded-For is believed.

        * [native] Wrong enable secrets are counted per source address:
          after enable_lockout_after of them within enable_lockout_s (5
          in 60 s by default; 0 = off) that address's enable is refused
          for the window, whatever sends them.  door.enable_lockouts and
          door.enable_refused count it.

=========================== Release 0.6.5 ==============================

2026-10-15  Yury Kirsanov
        * [upgrade] The staged builds are ordered by their file time at the
          file system's resolution; two staged within one second were
          told apart arbitrarily and the prune could drop the newer.

        * [write-through] A copy of a through-written record that reaches
          a member from a peer is marked as Redis-held on arrival.  The
          mark used to travel as a second record in a push of its own,
          and an invalidation landing between the two left a copy that
          the owned count and the upfill took for the fleet's own.
          write_through.copies_marked counts the marks written this way.

2026-10-14  Yury Kirsanov
        * [cluster] The settings a fleet must agree on travel with each
          member's keepalive: [wal] fsync, whether an HTTP token is set,
          the [alerts] thresholds, tombstone_retain_s and _max, and each
          read-through collection's upstream writes, prefixes, role and
          write-through settings.  A member holding one differently from
          the most common value is named on every node as a
          config.<setting> alert.  Members before 0.6.5 report none and
          are left out.

        * [cluster] The tombstone table's limits are alerts:
          cluster.tombstones when tombstone_retain_max drops deletes
          before tombstone_retain_s is up (a member away then would not
          be told of them), cluster.peer_absent when a member comes back
          after an absence longer than the deletes are kept.

        * [cluster] Every member reports its build: /members and the
          status page's cards show each node's version and wire epochs,
          and the header says "mixed versions" during a rolling upgrade.
          These are reported, never compared, so a roll raises no
          configuration alert.

        * [persistence] A node refuses to start on state a newer build
          wrote, and says so, instead of reading it as damage: a snapshot
          of a newer format (perfcached -R says newer-format), a WAL
          record of an unknown type (unless [wal] unknown_records = skip,
          and never when the loaded snapshot already covers it), or a
          cluster-id file of a newer version.  The WAL reader no longer
          stops at an unknown record type and drops every record after
          it; perfcached -W shows such records by type.

        * [read-through] upstream_drain_mb_rate (16 MB a second): the
          drain and upfill sweeps move at most that many MB of values a
          second beside upstream_drain_rate's keys, so a sweep of large
          values takes no more of the writer thread than one of small
          ones - about a quarter at the defaults.  upstream.drain and
          write_through.upfill report the bytes they moved.

        * [read-through] write_through.writer_busy_pct: the writer
          thread's share of the CPU over the last five minutes - one
          thread carries every write-through write of a collection - on
          the page's writes line, amber from 75%.

        * [read-through] upfill says what Redis's values replaced.  Where
          Redis already holds a key, its value replaces the fleet's on
          every member; the sweep now compares the two first and counts
          Redis's as the same, the same with another expiry, or different
          (upstream.write_through.upfill redis_same, redis_retimed and
          redis_differed), and names each key that differed in the log
          with both lengths and expiries - never a value - the first 100
          of a sweep.  The comparison runs on the sweep's own thread, not
          the writer's; compared_text, compared_walked and uncompared say
          how each verdict was reached, and a document too deep to walk
          counts as uncompared, never as different.  upfill <collection>
          check reads each key from the master and makes the same
          comparison, writing nothing.

        * [upgrade] A release tarball's detached ed25519 signature is
          verified against the release key built into the binary, and
          [upgrade] trusted_keys for builds you sign yourself:
          perfcached -K <tarball> checks one offline.

        * [upgrade] upgrade_stage receives a signed build in chunks (a
          privileged connection; perfcli upgrade-stage <tarball>
          <version>), verifies it whole, keeps it in the state directory
          and passes it to every member that can take one, each verifying
          it with its own keys.  upgrade_status lists what is staged and
          what each member answered.  At most two builds are kept, within
          [upgrade] stage_mb.

        * [upgrade] An optional root helper, perfcached-upgrade, with its
          systemd units (installed, not enabled): upgrade_switch asks it
          to install a staged build; it verifies the build with the
          binary already installed, unpacks it beside the live prefix,
          switches and restarts, and switches back if the new build does
          not report ready.  It reads the daemon's files as the daemon's
          own user.  Upgrades need [daemon] state_dir.  The Debian
          packages do not carry the helper.

=========================== Release 0.6.4 ==============================

2026-10-14  Yury Kirsanov
        * [cluster] A node's restart no longer empties the fleet's
          write-through cache.  A record a node drops for itself - on
          Redis's invalidation, in the startup flush, a mark found alone -
          was retained like a delete, and the sweep that replays retained
          deletes to a peer back from a restart replayed a whole startup
          flush: every member lost every record written through.  Such a
          drop is still retained to refuse an older copy, but is never
          replayed.

        * [read-through] Under upstream_writes = through, a node could
          answer nil for a key Redis held.  A write reaches the other
          members as the record and, separately, its no-fall mark, and
          Redis's broadcast invalidation of the same write reaches every
          member on its own; landing between the two, it left the mark
          without its record, and a mark told the node not to ask Redis.
          A read that finds a written-through key's mark alone now drops
          it and fetches Redis's value (write_through bare_marks), and an
          invalidation keeps a mark it finds without its record, so a
          record arriving after it is not counted as the fleet's own
          (fleet_owned).

2026-10-13  Yury Kirsanov
        * [cluster] The restart reconcile has a fast path.  A node that
          comes back from a restart asks a peer that was up throughout
          for every delete - and every expiry - made since its snapshot
          began, from the peer's retained deletes; when the peer can
          vouch for that whole window and the whole replay arrives, the
          pass ends in seconds instead of asking about every replayed
          key.  Otherwise (no snapshot loaded, a window older than
          tombstone_retain_s, the tombstone_retain_max cap dropped
          entries inside it, a peer older than 0.6.4) the node asks key
          by key as before, and stats.cluster.reconcile_fast_why says
          why.  [cluster] reconcile_fast = no turns it off.

        * [cluster] An eagerly replicated key that expires is now
          remembered with the retained deletes, at the version that
          expired: an older, longer-lived copy can no longer come back
          from a restarted node or be accepted from a peer.  Size
          tombstone_retain_max for deletes PLUS expiries a second, times
          tombstone_retain_s.

        * [cluster] Asking key by key goes out in slices ten times a
          second, up to a quarter of the free pending-request slots a
          second, between 64 and reconcile_rate_max (1,024); a refused
          slot or a timed-out pull holds it at 64 for 10 s.  stats and
          /metrics carry how far an open pass has got (walked, total,
          rate, seconds left) and the status page shows it on the node's
          card and in the reconcile row.

        * [read-through] Under upstream_writes = through the native door
          forwards add, sub, hcmd writes and mset (one MSET); rlhit is
          refused, as RL.HIT already was.

        * [read-through] upstream_write_user / upstream_write_password:
          the writer's own Redis credential, so readers need no write
          rights.

        * [read-through] The keys a collection owned before it switched
          to through are counted (write_through.fleet_owned) and can be
          filled to the master with NX (upfill), a PEXPIRE only where the
          NX applied; hashes are skipped.

        * [read-through] The writer sends a batch of plain writes in one
          round and reads the answers in order (write_through.pipelined).

        * [alerts] host.memory is measured beside the huge-page pool:
          memory the pool holds is not counted as used, and perfcached's
          part of the pool is named in the text.  node.reconcile: a
          reconcile with no live witness.

        * [page] A member whose reconcile has no witness blinks red and
          says to restart it; one reconciling with a witness blinks
          amber.  With nothing active the alerts table says so.  The
          upstream rows' icons are drawn, centred on the row.

        * 0.6.3 was not published: it is part of this release.

=========================== Release 0.6.3 ==============================

2026-10-13  Yury Kirsanov
        * [read-through] Under upstream_writes = through every node
          follows every change Redis makes under the collection's
          prefixes (broadcast tracking, CLIENT TRACKING ON BCAST with a
          PREFIX per upstream_prefixes pattern that is a prefix): a write
          another Redis client makes to a key written through here is
          seen - the node drops its record and the next read fetches
          Redis's value.  Keys outside upstream_prefixes are never
          dropped.  A lost tracking connection drops every key written
          through on that node and keeps the keys the fleet owned before
          the switch to through.  A server refusing broadcast tracking
          raises the warning write.<col>.tracking.

        * [read-through] The write-through queue is bounded:
          upstream_write_queue (10,000) writes may wait for the master;
          past it a write is refused at once, not applied.  A write's
          upstream_timeout_ms runs from its queueing, and one with under
          a quarter of it left is answered "not applied" without being
          sent - "may or may not have been applied" now only for a write
          that was sent.  The warning write.<col>.queue says either
          happened.

        * [read-through] A delete Redis confirms under through, on an
          eagerly replicated collection, sends its tombstone to the fleet
          even from a node that did not hold the key, so a copy of it
          still on its way from another node is refused instead of
          keeping a key Redis no longer has.

        * [docs] The RESP and HTTP doors stay plaintext: said in the
          README's known limits and the GUIDE, with the per-node
          write-through ceiling.

=========================== Release 0.6.2 ==============================

2026-10-13  Yury Kirsanov
        * [read-through] upstream_writes = through: every client write on
          the collection goes to the upstream's master first - the
          listed upstream whose ROLE says master, the one the serving
          replicas follow when two say so - and its keys are read back
          in the same round trip, so the fleet then holds what Redis
          holds (a value with Redis's expiry; a key gone as gone; a list,
          set, sorted set or stream left to Redis).  The client gets
          Redis's own reply.  With no master every write is refused
          (critical alert write.<col>) and reads go on.  At the RESP door
          every write command is forwarded as sent (EVAL, EVALSHA and
          RL.HIT are refused); at the native door set, setnx, setxx, del,
          expire, jset and jdel.  JSON writes need RedisJSON on the
          master, asked when the writer connects (warning
          write.<col>.json).

        * [read-through] upstream_unknown = pass (the default under
          through): a command perfcached does not know is classed by
          Redis (COMMAND INFO, COMMAND GETKEYS) and passed - a write to
          the master with its keys read back, a read to a serving
          replica - and Redis's reply relayed byte for byte up to
          upstream_pass_max_kb (1024); larger is refused, never cut.
          Admin, dangerous, blocking, pub/sub, transaction and script
          commands, another database, keyless writes and keys outside
          upstream_prefixes are refused whatever Redis says.  refuse
          keeps "unknown command".

        * [read-through] The writer's checks never follow the traffic:
          the master is looked for at start and after a failure (backing
          off to every 10 s), ROLE asked every 10 s, RedisJSON every 60 s
          while missing.  /stats upstream.write_through and the page's
          read-through line show the target, its state and what was
          forwarded in the last five minutes.  Pub/sub is not bridged.

        * [read-through] Write-through stats: by command as sent to Redis
          (applied, not applied, Redis errors, failed, average), the last
          eight errors with Redis's own words and the key, and the
          slowest write in five minutes - in /stats and on the page.

        * [stats] A command that waited for an upstream (a read-through
          miss, a write-through write) is counted once in the commands
          table; it was counted twice.

        * [http] A browser can open a page behind [secrets] http: the
          401 asks for its login, and Basic with the token as the
          password is accepted beside Bearer.

        * [http] http_rate (20 requests a second per client address,
          loopback exempt) answers a faster address 429 Too Many
          Requests; http_cache_ms (1000) builds /stats and /metrics at
          most once in that long for any number of readers.  /stats
          http counts both.

        * [page] The slow log and the alerts fold like the commands
          table; the commands table sorts by calls or by its slowest
          call; a read-through collection's upstreams and writes are one
          named group under its row; every card holds its content at
          desktop widths.

        * [http] A peer refused by http_allow can no longer stall a
          worker: its 403 is sent and its socket drained without waiting.
          A silent refused connection held a worker for 200 ms, and one
          sending a byte every 150 ms held it indefinitely.

=========================== Release 0.6.1 ==============================

2026-10-13  Yury Kirsanov
        * [alerts] Alert mail a person can read.  The subject is
          "<STATE>: <title> on <host>" - CRITICAL, WARNING, INFO or
          RESOLVED - in at most 78 characters, and From names the host.
          The mail has a plain and an HTML part: per alert what happened,
          its status, when it started, what the node does by itself and
          what to do.  A resolve, or a problem mailed again because it
          got worse, answers the problem's first mail.  Levels say how
          soon to act: a clean start is info (not mailed at the default
          level), an unclean start and a WAL loss are warnings, the
          third loss within an hour and a heal stuck for ten minutes are
          critical.

        * [page] An io card: which backend runs (io_uring or epoll),
          how, a fallback in amber with its reason, and the busiest
          worker's share of the last five minutes.  /stats io.workers[]
          and /metrics perfcached_io_worker_busy_seconds_total.

        * [alerts] A node on epoll whose busiest worker stays over
          [alerts] epoll_busy_pct (80) for five minutes is warned, with
          the change to make: io = io_uring where the host can run it,
          else what refused it.  -C and the start log say whether
          io_uring would run under io = epoll.

        * [read-through] Several upstreams for one collection, up to
          eight.  upstream_role = replica uses only servers whose ROLE
          says replica (upstream_master_fallback = yes lets the master
          serve while none does); upstream_mode = balance sends each
          fetch to the one with the fewest in flight, failover to one
          active upstream (upstream_primary), the next taking over when
          it stops and the primary returning after
          upstream_preempt_hold_s (upstream_preempt).  Each upstream has
          its own tracking; one lost drops only its own copies.  The page
          shows a link per upstream.

        * [read-through] The upstream password is the collection's,
          upstream_password, so one perfcached can front several Redis
          servers, a collection each.  [secrets] upstream is refused
          with that advice.

        * [read-through] No ring of read-throughs: an upstream that is
          itself a perfcached is refused unless upstream_allow_perfcached
          = yes, and then a key crosses one perfcached hop at most.  Both
          ends warn.

        * [page] The hit-rate bar stays put whatever its caption says.

        * [pubsub] A subscriber that keeps up is no longer closed as slow
          when its worker drains a large backlog of big messages at once
          on io_uring: a drain stops at 1 MB as well as at 1,024
          messages, so the sends in flight complete between batches.

        * [page] The hit rate's "now" is the last 30 s of the page's own
          polls, "hit rate (30 s)": over one 3 s poll, a collection read
          every few seconds flipped between "no reads" and "0.0%".
          A read-through collection's entries count the copies it
          fetched, and its <name>.nofall companion is named on its
          read-through line instead of listed as a collection.

        * [alerts] A master listed under upstream_role = replica is skipped
          by design (it is listed so the collection follows a failover):
          the page shows it greyed and no alert is raised for it.

        * [page] A read-through link's share is labelled "% of fetches"; a
          serving link with no fetches in five minutes says so; a
          replica's "lag" is the seconds since it last heard from its
          master, as Redis reports it.

=========================== Release 0.6.0.1 ============================

2026-10-12  Yury Kirsanov
        * [io] An io_uring worker waiting for the network is no longer
          counted as in iowait.  It always has requests pending - every
          connection's receive - so an idle node read close to 100%
          iowait and PSI io pressure.  Kernels 6.15 and later are told
          not to count it; on an older kernel perfcached -C, the start
          log and /stats io.backend.counts_iowait say that it is counted
          and is not disk I/O.

        * [alerts] Alarms when the host's CPU, the device holding the WAL
          or the host's memory run high, and the arena at the same
          levels: a warning at 65%, critical at 75%, changed in a new
          [alerts] section.  CPU and disk are averaged over the last
          minute and raised after five minutes over, memory and the
          arena after one; each clears five points under its level.
          The disk is measured as iostat's %util, never from iowait.
          /stats alerts.host shows what is measured.  The arena alarm
          was a warning under 10% left and critical under 1%.

        * [pubsub] On io_uring, a subscriber that fell behind while a send
          to it was in flight could be closed as a failed write - not
          counted as a slow subscriber and logged "write error".  The
          output cap now counts the send in flight, so it is closed as
          the slow subscriber it is, counted and logged so.

        * [page] "daemon cpu" is a share of the CPUs the daemon may use
          (/stats process.cpus, /metrics perfcached_process_cpus), with
          the share of one core beside it; it was a share of one core.

        * [docs] PRODUCTION.md: iowait on io_uring before kernel 6.15;
          the high-water alarms.

=========================== Release 0.6.0 ==============================

2026-10-11  Yury Kirsanov
        * [io] io_uring by default.  The workers receive into provided
          buffers with one multishot receive per connection and send their
          replies with the turn's single io_uring_enter() - no read() and
          no write() a request.  At start the daemon tries io_uring for
          real and falls back to epoll where the kernel (before 6.0),
          kernel.io_uring_disabled or a container's seccomp profile refuses
          it; the reason is logged, shown in /stats io.backend, and
          perfcached -C says beforehand.  [daemon] io = epoll forces epoll.
          On 16-vCPU VMs with multiqueue NICs, against epoll: 41% more
          RESP and 63% more native requests a second one at a time, 12%
          more pipelined, a third less CPU a request.

        * [io] epoll: a short read ends a wakeup's reads - one read() a
          request instead of two.

        * [clients] A client whose pipelined replies outrun its socket is
          throttled, not closed: at 4 MB of replies unread its further
          requests wait and its socket is not read, until they drain below
          1 MB.  One that reads nothing for output_stall_s (default 30)
          meanwhile is closed as a slow consumer.  The log says
          "throttling client <ip:port>" the first time and the disconnect
          line counts the throttles; /stats clients.output and /metrics
          count them.  A slow consumer's close now says so (it read "peer
          closed").

        * [io] Opt-in busy polling per worker: busy_poll_us and
          busy_poll_prefer (kernel 6.9+), off by default.  Its effect on
          perfcached is not measured yet.

        * [stats] /stats io and /metrics: the system calls against the work
          they moved - waits, reads and writes, io_uring enters,
          completions, receives and sends, UDP datagrams a call - and each
          worker's I/O (perfcached_io_worker_ops_total).

        * [docs] PRODUCTION.md: one NIC queue per CPU on the nodes and the
          clients, irqbalance, the daemon unpinned, io_uring.

=========================== Release 0.5.9 ==============================

2026-10-11  Yury Kirsanov
        * [alerts] A node raises its own alerts: failed or healing, records
          the WAL lost, a slow fsync, writes refused for want of memory, an
          arena near its ceiling, a read-through upstream unreachable or
          its tracking off, its own start (critical after an unclean stop).
          Each is logged, kept (the last 100 in /stats alerts and on the
          status page's alerts card) and counted on /metrics.

        * [mail] With [mail] and a build with an SMTP client (PC_MAIL=
          libcurl - the release packages have one), alerts are mailed:
          STARTTLS or TLS, AUTH, the password in [secrets] mail; the same
          alert at most once per min_interval_s unless it got worse,
          everything within digest_s in one mail, the clear when it
          recovers.  `perfcli -E ... mailtest` sends a test mail and prints
          the server's answer.  tls_verify = no keeps TLS for a relay whose
          certificate cannot be verified (a self-signed or expired one) and
          skips the check, with a warning at every config check.

        * [cluster] In a fleet, [mail] on one node is enough: the nodes
          elect the lowest-numbered one that can mail, relay their alerts
          to it, and what happens to the fleet itself - a member silent,
          failed, gone or missing, the fleet below a majority - is mailed
          once, by that node.  The page says where this node's mail goes.

        * [libperfd] 0.6.0: perfd_get_buf, perfd_jget_buf and
          perfd_hget_buf read into the caller's buffer and allocate
          nothing.  The SONAME stays libperfd.so.0.

        * [build] make menuconfig chooses a build flavor - release, debug
          or asan (sanitizers linked statically) - and a non-release build
          says so in -V, /stats and the page header.
          contrib/systemd/cores.conf keeps a crashed node's core;
          asan.conf routes a sanitizer's report beside it.

        * [packages] perfcached and perfcached-standalone depend on
          libcurl (the alert mail).

=========================== Release 0.5.8 ==============================

2026-10-11  Yury Kirsanov
        * [libperfd] 0.5.0: a shared library, libperfd.so.0, beside the
          static archive - an update with the same SONAME replaces it in
          place.  The ABI carries what that needs: perfd_opts starts with
          its size (PERFD_OPTS_INIT, perfd_opts_init()), errors carry a
          kind (perfd_error_kind()), values are released with
          perfd_free_value(), the library logs through perfd_set_log(),
          and only perfd_* is exported.  A pkg-config file, libperfd.pc;
          make install-lib.  The archive is position-independent, so it
          links into shared objects such as plugins and modules.

        * [packages] libperfd0 and libperfd-dev .deb; libperfd and
          libperfd-dev .apk for Alpine, signed with the release key.

        * [licence] libperfd's archive no longer carries a GPL object from
          the daemon.  The library's MIT boundary is now the set of files
          the compiler reads to build it, checked on every push
          (test/mitboundtest.sh); tools/sync-libperfd.sh, which exported
          the source for consumers to copy, is retired.

        * [asterisk] func_perfd links the installed libperfd (found by
          configure through pkg-config, or --with-perfd=DIR) instead of
          compiling a copy: the Asterisk patch goes from 22 files to 8, and
          the module compiles under developer mode.  Its build refuses a
          copy of libperfd's source in funcs/perfd.

        * [test] test/abitest.sh holds libperfd.so to its SONAME: a caller
          built against the frozen 0.5.0 header runs against the new
          library, and no exported symbol disappears.

=========================== Release 0.5.7.1 ============================

2026-10-10  Yury Kirsanov
        * [cluster] A node refuses a restart only when it is the LAST live
          witness of a peer's reconcile.  0.5.7 refused while any peer
          reconciled; a reconcile with two witnesses loses nothing when
          one restarts.  A reconciling node names its only live witness
          in its keepalive (older members ignore the field, and a 0.5.7
          member that does not say it is waited for as before).
          stats.cluster.reconcile_witnesses_live.

        * [page] The header says when a reconcile is in trouble: red
          "reconcile has no witness - restart this node", amber "do not
          restart: node N needs it to finish its reconcile"; the fleet
          chip names another member whose reconcile has no witness.
          /members carries each member's reconciling and sole_witness.

        * [test] Two cluster suites waited only for their nodes to see
          each other, not for them to be ready, and now and then probed
          a node still starting up.

=========================== Release 0.5.7 ==============================

2026-10-10  Yury Kirsanov
        * [clients] The memory guard no longer counts a huge-page arena
          twice.  A hugetlb pool is allocated at boot and never counted
          in MemAvailable, yet the guard also subtracted the arena's
          uncommitted part from it: on a 2 GB node with arena_mb = 1024
          on a 512-page pool it refused every new client once the arena
          had given memory back.  Under a meminfo limit the pool now
          comes off MemTotal; a hugetlb arena's uncommitted part counts
          only as surplus pages the kernel may make beyond the pool's
          free ones (vm.nr_overcommit_hugepages).  Boot says when a pool
          is outside the limit; /stats clients.memory.hugetlb_pool.

        * [upstream] EXISTS, TTL, PTTL and TYPE of a key the collection
          does not hold read no value: one round trip of TYPE and PTTL
          answers them, and a hash or a JSON document is remembered as
          PRESENT - its type and the upstream's TTL - for the next ones.
          A read of the key then fetches the value; client tracking
          drops the mark as it drops a copy.  It used to fetch and store
          the whole document, a second round trip, for a probe.  The
          native door's exists also answers true for a key held as a
          fetched copy (it said false).  /stats upstream.fill_present,
          present_hits; the status page counts them among the lookups.

        * [clients] A connect storm no longer stands between a worker and
          its established clients.  A worker turn runs at most 8 Noise
          handshakes and parks the rest, oldest first, out of the ready
          list, and accepts at most 64 connections; one worker at ~6,000
          encrypted connects a second: an established client's p99 92-107
          -> 33-37 ms.  /stats clients.handshakes.

        * [cluster] A node does not call itself safe to restart while a
          peer's reconcile is still running - it may be the only witness
          that peer has left.  stats.cluster.restart.waits_for names the
          peers.  A reconcile left with no witness at all (every peer
          restarted after the node went down) says so once, after 30
          seconds, with the remedy, and counts the time in
          stats.cluster.reconcile_no_witness_s.  A rolling restart that
          went on regardless used to leave the first node's reconcile
          waiting for good.

        * [docs] GUIDE: rolling restarts by restart.safe and waits_for;
          how long to believe "absent" when the upstream's tracking runs.

=========================== Release 0.5.6.6 ============================

2026-10-10  Yury Kirsanov
        * [clients] An encrypted native client past max_clients or the
          memory floor is refused at once, before the Noise handshake, with
          the RESP door's "-ERR max number of clients reached": a storm of
          refused clients no longer costs a handshake each (12,000 clients
          at a limit of 2,000: a third less daemon CPU).  A library that
          does not read the line sees its handshake fail, as on any close.
          A plaintext native door (loopback, unix) still answers an error
          frame to the first request.

        * [libperfd] 0.4.2: fits a musl thread's 128 KB default stack.  A
          connect from a thread on Alpine overflowed it - the handshake
          kept two 64 KB buffers on the stack for messages of ~50 bytes;
          they are sized to the handshake now, and the transport record
          lives with the handle.

=========================== Release 0.5.6.5 ============================

2026-10-09  Yury Kirsanov
        * [clients] A memory guard keeps the out-of-memory killer away from
          the daemon.  Once a second it reads the memory the node may still
          use - MemAvailable, or the headroom of a memory-limited cgroup it
          runs in (v2 or v1), whichever is tighter - less the arena's
          uncommitted ceiling.  While less than the floor remains, new
          client connections are refused exactly as at max_clients
          ("-ERR max number of clients reached" on RESP, an error frame on
          the native door); open connections and /stats are untouched, and
          refusing stops once a further 5% of the limit is free.
          [daemon] memory_floor = N% (default 15%, kept within 128 MB ..
          4 GB) | N MB | N GB | off.  On a 1 GB node 50,000 idle RESP
          connections had the daemon killed; a 300 MB cgroup now holds
          24,457 of 25,000 and refuses the rest.

        * [clients] max_clients, unset, counts memory as well as
          descriptors: the smaller of the two, the boot line and
          clients.max_basis say which.  A configured max_clients above what
          the memory holds stands, with a warning - the guard protects.

        * [clients] An idle connection holds no buffers: after two quiet
          seconds its input and output buffers are released (they grow back
          on its next request) - 14 KB to 2.2 KB of memory an idle client.
          Once a burst of released or closed connections has settled, the
          freed heap is handed back to the system (glibc's allocator), so
          the daemon's memory falls back after a mass disconnect.

        * [perf] A busy client is no longer slowed by idle ones: every
          request used to pay a walk over all of its worker's connections
          (20,000 idle: 60 -> 750 us a PING; now unchanged).

        * [stats] /stats clients.memory and clients.buffers; /metrics
          perfcached_memory_limit_bytes, _available_bytes, _floor_bytes,
          perfcached_memory_refusing, perfcached_clients_refused_memory_total,
          perfcached_conn_buffers_released_total, perfcached_heap_trims_total;
          the page shows a red "memory floor" chip while refusing.

        * [page] The clients card shows connects per second and the
          5-minute average; the door cards say "connects (5 min)".

        * [libperfd] 0.4.1: a blocking connect takes the key from the
          process-wide cache (it derived it every time, 150 ms and 64 MB
          each); the derive is single-flight across threads, and
          perfd_psk_prepare() derives before fork so a forking server's
          children inherit it.

=========================== Release 0.5.6.4 ============================

2026-10-09  Yury Kirsanov
        * [stats] /stats and the status page count from the last reset,
          and the reset now runs on a schedule: daily at local midnight by
          default ([daemon] stats_reset = off | 1d .. 7d, stats_reset_at =
          HH:MM; 7d resets every Monday), so no count they show grows
          without end.  Every node resets at the same local moment and
          reports it as since.reset_at; since.next_reset says when the next
          one is.  PERFCACHED_STATS_RESET in the environment overrides
          stats_reset.

        * [stats] The status page's "reset stats" resets the whole fleet:
          the node and every live peer, all as of the same moment, so the
          fleet figures the page shows count from one time.  CONFIG
          RESETSTAT and perfcli reset-stats still reset one node.  The
          read-through counters are reset with the rest.

        * [metrics] /metrics is never reset.  A stats reset used to drop the
          collection hit, miss, store and expired counters and the
          unknown-command counters to zero there, which Prometheus reads as
          a counter reset; they now count from the daemon's start, across
          resets and collection resizes.

        * [metrics] perfcached_command_latency_seconds has one bucket per
          octave (3 us .. 262 ms, and +Inf): 18 rows per command instead of
          129, which were most of each scrape.  Every bound is an edge of
          the daemon's own histogram, so the counts are exact.

        * [stats] The per-door connection and request totals no longer read
          negative once they pass 4,294,967,295 after a reset: they are
          counted to 64 bits.

=========================== Release 0.5.6.3 ============================

2026-10-09  Yury Kirsanov
        * [daemon] A crash on the metrics door is fixed: a worker could
          use a connection it had already freed.  The worker closes
          metrics connections that have been open longer than
          http_timeout without a complete request, and it did so after
          collecting a batch of socket events but before handling them;
          a connection that reached its timeout in the very moment its
          request (or its close) arrived was freed and then handed its
          event - a use-after-free that ended in SIGSEGV in conn_destroy.
          Read-through completions destroy connections from inside a
          batch the same way.  A destroyed connection is now closed at
          once but freed only after the batch, and an event for it is
          ignored.  Found on a test fleet polled by a monitor and a
          Prometheus scraper; 0.5.6, 0.5.6.1 and 0.5.6.2 are affected.

=========================== Release 0.5.6.2 ============================

2026-10-08  Yury Kirsanov
        * [page] Every count on the status page is the last five
          minutes, and the daemon keeps the window: /stats publishes
          win_<field> beside each counter the page shows (one snapshot a
          minute, the window 4-5 minutes, since.win_s saying which), and
          the daemon builds its own stats once a minute so the window is
          true from the moment the page opens.  Figures of how much there
          is (entries, held, open connections) stay current; maxima
          (fsync, pending) are the last five minutes' maxima; a reset or a
          restarted member counts from its restart.  The collections
          table's fleet and per-node figures were lifetime totals under a
          "last 5 min" heading.

        * [page] The header counts the fleet: "fleet 3/3 ready", or how
          many are ready of how many there should be and who is not - a
          member that left or is missing from the list altogether no
          longer leaves the header looking healthy.  /members carries the
          fleet's size ([cluster] expect, or the most members seen).

        * [page] The collections table fits its panel at 1280 px and
          wider; a long collection name wraps instead of pushing the
          table under a scrollbar.

        * [page] The WAL card's fsync times read in us, ms or s, and the
          headline hit rate is the caches' - a read-through collection,
          mostly lookups of keys its upstream does not have, gets its own
          figure: how much of it was answered without the upstream.

        * [upstream] upstream_writes = refuse: a read-through collection
          clients can only read.  Every write to it, on every door, is
          refused with one message, and so are a drain sweep and a
          restore into it; reads, the negative cache and tracking are
          unchanged.

        * [collections] One rule for a collection's name wherever one is
          made - the config, create, rename and a member's announce: 1-32
          characters of A-Z a-z 0-9 _ -, not starting with "-".  A name
          from before the rule is kept where it is and named in the log.

        * [native] A client that has no secret is told so at once: its
          first request, sent in the clear to a door that needs the
          handshake, was taken for a long handshake and left waiting
          until the client gave up.  perfcli also reads
          /etc/perfcached.conf for its target and secret.

        * [core] A record moved back into its bucket from the overflow
          chain expires on time; it could stay until something else
          expired beside it.

=========================== Release 0.5.6.1 ============================

2026-10-07  Yury Kirsanov
        * [upstream] The drain sweep reads untracked.  Its connection
          asked the upstream for client tracking like a fetching one,
          although what it reads goes straight into the collection:
          the upstream then remembered every swept key for it and sent
          an invalidation for each later write to any of them, for
          nothing.

        * [upstream] A read-through collection's companion
          (<name>.nofall) takes its parent's autoscale as well as its
          size, so one buckets_log2 and one floor size the pair - under
          the default it shrank while empty and grew back during a
          sweep.

        * [upstream] Read-through on the RESP door works under a mapped
          db index.  With resp_collections mapping SELECT n onto a named
          collection, a read of a key only the upstream held answered nil
          without fetching: the gate looked for an upstream on "n", not
          on the collection n maps to.

        * [upstream] A read-through collection counts what its clients
          got.  A read answered from a fetched copy was counted a MISS of
          the collection - twice, with the gate's own probe - and every
          lookup counted another miss on the companion (<name>.nofall):
          the status page showed 0 hits and only misses for a collection
          serving its clients correctly.  A read answered with a value is
          now a hit, an absent answer a miss, and the read-through's own
          looks count nowhere.  The status page adds a line under each
          read-through collection: lookups split into fetched from the
          upstream (found / absent, average fetch time), answered from
          fetched copies, and answered absent from the negative cache,
          with the copies held.

=========================== Release 0.5.6 ==============================

2026-10-07  Yury Kirsanov
        * [daemon] "perfcached ready" is logged once every worker's
          listeners are open.  The workers open their own listeners after
          the main thread starts them, and the line did not wait for them:
          a client that connected the moment it appeared could be refused.

        * [upstream] Read-through tracks the upstream's changes only on
          one server.  A fetching connection asks Redis to send its
          invalidations to the tracking connection by that connection's
          client id, and a client id means something only on the server
          that issued it: behind a round-robin proxy in front of several
          replicas, the id could be another client's there, and a copy
          believed tracked would never see a change.  Each connection now
          reads its server's run_id (INFO server); one that reached
          another server than the tracking connection is not tracked -
          its copies live at most upstream_negative_ms, stats count it
          (upstream.tracking_other_server) and the log says so.  An
          upstream user that may not run INFO gets no tracking: allow
          +info (the GUIDE's ACL line has it).  stats show the tracking
          server as upstream.server.

        * [wal] Standalone edition: a key that expired and was reaped,
          then written again on another worker thread, could come back
          after a crash with its expiry instead of the new value.  A
          thread's version clock is its own there, and the expiry sweep
          (and a resize copy dropping a lapsed record) removed the record
          without carrying its version forward, so the new write could be
          stamped below it and the WAL replay, by version, preferred the
          expired one.  A reap now publishes the reaped version as a
          delete does.  In every standalone release since 0.4.0; the
          clustered edition is not affected.

        * [wal] A node holding no records that was restarted more times
          than its WAL has segments came back FAILED ("wal: FULL at
          startup") and refused writes, and every restart after that did
          the same.  The snapshot it had just loaded covered the old
          records, but the start-up check counted only snapshots taken
          by the running process.  It now counts the loaded one.

        * [page] A host in trouble is seen at the top of the status page.
          The header's state blinks red (failed) or amber (any other
          state but ready), as the node's card does, and a chip beside
          it names the other hosts in trouble - "fleet: node 2 failed,
          node 3 recovering" - blinking at the worst one's colour and
          linking to the fleet.  Held steady under prefers-reduced-motion.

        * [test] tools/redis-replica-interop.sh: read-through against a
          real Redis replica in throwaway containers - the master's
          changes arriving as the replica's invalidations, a replica
          restart, a round-robin proxy over master and replica, a user
          without +info.

=========================== Release 0.5.5 ==============================

2026-10-07  Yury Kirsanov
        * [upstream] Read-through from a Redis upstream.  A collection
          may name an upstream - Redis, Valkey or another perfcached -
          that a node reads on a local miss and never writes, so a
          fleet can take a keyspace over from Redis without copying it
          first.  Only keys matching upstream_prefixes fall through.
          Strings, hashes and RedisJSON documents are carried; a list,
          set, sorted set or stream answers WRONGTYPE.  A fetched copy
          is node-local and keeps the upstream's TTL, and Redis client
          tracking drops it when the upstream changes the key.  A
          change (INCR, HSET, DEL, EXPIRE and the rest) first takes the
          upstream's value over as a client write, logged and
          replicated; a key the fleet has written or deleted never
          reads through again - a companion collection, <name>.nofall,
          remembers it.  On the RESP and the native door; proxy, shard
          and spread collections refuse an upstream.  perfcached sends
          the upstream only the handshake and reads.

        * [upstream] The drain sweep: perfcli -E <enable> updrain
          <collection> reads every key the upstream holds under the
          collection's patterns and takes over each one the fleet does
          not already hold, has not written and has not deleted, at
          most upstream_drain_rate keys a second (default 5000).
          Progress is in stats as upstream.drain.

        * [test] Suites for read-through on one node and in an eager
          fleet, against a scripted upstream that fails the run on any
          write; tools/redis-interop.sh runs the same paths against a
          throwaway redis:8 container.

=========================== Release 0.5.1 ==============================

2026-10-06  Yury Kirsanov
        * [cluster] A node's identity is derived from the machine by
          default - the DMI product uuid, or inside a container (where
          that uuid is the host's) a NIC's MAC - keyed by a salt the node
          mints once into its state directory (node-salt).  Two daemons
          on one machine are two members, a moved state directory keeps
          its identity, and a disk cloned onto another machine derives
          another one.  An identity an earlier release minted at random
          is kept, with a WARNING naming the source the machine offers
          and how to switch.  The shipped systemd units copy the
          root-only DMI uuid into the runtime directory for the non-root
          daemon.

        * [store] autoscale = floor: grows by key count as auto does, and
          shrinks only down to the size the collection was given - its
          buckets_log2, a create's size or an explicit resize - so an
          empty collection sized for the keys it expects keeps that
          size.  /stats carries autoscale_floor_log2.

        * [cluster] A departing master's goodbye is no longer taken for a
          UDP block.  Its members measured its silence from the
          machine's boot and, while its TCP lane still answered during
          its shutdown snapshot, moved their traffic to it onto TCP for
          good.  A forced lane now comes back: the node asks the peer by
          UDP every [cluster] lane_retry_s (default 30) and returns to
          UDP once the answer proves both directions (/stats
          cluster.lane.udp_restored).  A peer that leaves takes its
          forced lane with it.

        * [store] A SCAN, a snapshot or a resize copy that walked a
          table's overflow leg while the leg drained into the buckets
          could miss a record: the walk found its place again by
          position after records ahead of it had left.  It now resumes
          from the last record it handed over, still in the chain.

        * [test] Suites for the identity sources, the floor mode and the
          lane's release, and a shim that holds a departing node's
          shutdown snapshot open.

=========================== Release 0.5.0 ==============================

2026-10-06  Yury Kirsanov
        * [build] Alpine Linux (musl) is a supported runtime.  On Alpine
          3.24 (musl 1.2.6, its own malloc) the daemon serves 0.78-0.95
          of the glibc build's rate where the server is the bottleneck,
          and is ahead of redis:8-alpine on every measured row - INCR,
          SET/GET of 256 B and 1 KB values, a 7:1 mix, JSON.SET and
          JSON.GET, at 4 and 12 workers - by 1.5x to 14.9x.
          bench/Containerfile.alpine builds a 55 MB image.  CI builds
          both editions on Alpine and runs the standalone suites there.

        * [build] The allocator is a build choice: make menuconfig, or
          PC_ALLOC = libc | mimalloc | jemalloc.  libc stays the default
          everywhere; mimalloc means major version 2 (3.0.1 measured
          slower on the JSON path).  perfcached -V names a non-default
          allocator, and /stats carries process.allocator.

        * [json] No heap on the per-request JSON path: JSON.SET, GET,
          NUMINCRBY, ARRAPPEND, DEL and the hash forwards allocate
          nothing after warm-up (three to four allocations each before;
          on musl one of them was a 256 KB buffer mapped and unmapped on
          every command, which held JSON.SET at 25-30k a second - it now
          runs at 1.3M at 4 workers).

        * [libperfd] 0.4.0.  Nothing is allocated per call on a warm
          handle: a typed call's reply is borrowed until the handle's
          next call (perfd_get and perfd_jget still return a malloc'd
          result, as documented).  perfd_set_if and perfd_del_if, the
          compare-and-set and compare-and-delete below.  A route_hints
          option for proxy collections (below).  perfd.c is split into
          parts it includes - one translation unit, the same code.

        * [resp] SET ... IFEQ <value> | IFNE <value>, and DELEX <key>
          [IFEQ|IFNE <value>], in Redis 8.4's spelling and with its
          replies.  IFDEQ/IFDNE are refused by name: perfcached keeps no
          value digest.  Native methods setifeq, setifne, delifeq,
          delifne.

        * [resp] EVAL, EVALSHA and SCRIPT LOAD | EXISTS | FLUSH for the
          lock scripts of Symfony's Lock component (5.4 to 7.4): they are
          recognised by SHA1 and run natively, as one atomic step on the
          key's holder.  Any other script is refused.

        * [cluster] Proxy mode: a request may ask where its key lives
          (an opt-in flag), and the reply says which node holds it;
          libperfd's route_hints remembers that and sends the next
          request for the key straight there.

        * [cluster] Proxy mode: a GET, DEL or EXPIRE that a stale
          locator steered to a node no longer holding the key now asks
          the fleet once.  Before, the GET answered nil, the DEL answered
          0 and left the key alive on its new holder, and the EXPIRE left
          it without a TTL.

        * [perf] A SET that rewrites an unchanged value compares the two
          a word at a time and prefetches the stored one (on musl, SET
          of 256 B +7%, of 1 KB +15%); the reply writer's small appends
          and its numbers no longer go through memcpy (on musl a GET hit
          made seven memcpy calls, now three).

        * [test] Alpine in CI; allocation-counting suites for the JSON
          path and for libperfd; suites for the compare-and-set verbs,
          the Symfony lock scripts, holder hints and a stale proxy
          locator.

=========================== Release 0.4.6 ==============================

2026-10-05  Yury Kirsanov
        * [cluster] A forwarded hash READ is rendered on the asking node:
          the owner sends the record, not a reply tree.  The reply tree
          had to fit the forward ack (58,000 bytes) and the owner refused
          a larger one silently, so a hash the store accepts - 16+16-byte
          fields from 1,381, 1+1 from 4,833 - read on its owner and
          answered "holder rejected" one hop away.  The record is at most
          58,000 bytes by its own rule, so it always fits; the asking
          node keeps the command's arguments and renders the same reply
          the owner would have.  The new plane is used only when every
          node in the fleet advertises it: a fleet with a 0.4.5 member
          asks the old way, so 0.4.6 is a rolling restart.  Writes keep
          the old plane.

        * [cluster] A hash command over 58,000 bytes of arguments is
          refused by name on the owner as well as through a forwarder,
          with the same words; before, it ran on the owner and failed one
          hop away.  An owner that cannot ship a reply now counts
          fwd_reply_too_big in the cluster stats and logs a warning with
          the size.

        * [resp] A forwarded hash reply of 343 or more nils ("bad reply")
          converts to RESP now: the sink sized its text buffer at twice
          the tree, and a nil is one tree byte and five of RESP.

        * [hash] Hash reads take the road GET takes - a seqlock copy-out,
          no stripe mutex - and hash commands allocate nothing on their
          path: the per-command copies and the two 58 KB write buffers
          are gone (a per-thread edit scratch, taken once).  An HSET that
          creates a hash over an expired key no longer inherits the stale
          expiry that left the new hash expired-as-absent on its first
          read.  A hash record over its ceiling is refused by the
          validator wherever a record is read, and restore validates a
          hash record before storing it.

        * [cluster] The apply-thread stall detector stamped an item with
          the clock from BEFORE the take blocked, so the first item after
          an idle gap looked as old as the gap and a healthy ring read
          STALLED; a stale wait stamp no longer outlives an empty ring.
          The stats publish cluster.apply_rings[] (backlog, busy_ms,
          wait_ms).

        * [test] hashfwdtest (the forwarded reads, the ceiling, the
          feature gate with a real 0.4.5.2 member and the rolling
          restart), hashbench, clapplytest's stamp contract.

=========================== Release 0.4.5.2 ============================

2026-10-04  Yury Kirsanov
        * [release] 0.4.5.1 went red in CI on two suites and is superseded
          by this cut; the daemon is unchanged since 0.4.5.1.

        * [perfcli] A jget prints the document raw again: the fragment
          arrives as bytes of JSON text and the library's JSON edge rendered
          it as an escaped string.

        * [test] The suites that read frames by hand drop the moved stamp a
          parked reply now carries.  The two partition suites wait for a
          fresh fleet to be READY on every node before writing and name
          refused writes - under a loaded CI runner a 4 s pause was not
          enough, and seeing both peers is not READY yet - and reap
          the previous fleet before starting the next: every listener is
          SO_REUSEPORT, so a daemon started beside a dying one shares its
          ports with the corpse.  fwdtest's crash leg waited for nothing
          before killing the holder and compared the holder's own count
          across the restart: after a burst the rebalancer sheds records
          to the less-utilised peer a few MB per tick, so under a slow
          sanitizer job the count was still falling - moves in flight,
          read as crash loss.  The leg now waits for the rebalancer to
          settle before the barrier and judges durability on the fleet.  keysbudgettest's sparse-walk ceiling (32 turns a
          walk, calibrated natively) is 96 under a sanitizer build, where
          a 250 us turn covers fewer buckets; bucket-stepping stays at 128
          and is still proven every run.  walresidenttest paces its 64,000 writes on
          the WAL's staged count instead of racing the pump, fails its
          own positive control when a ring drop or a heal happened, and
          detects the drain on "nothing staged" rather than two equal
          byte counts - on a sanitizer runner the unpaced burst filled
          the ring and the run read 23 MB with thousands refused.

=========================== Release 0.4.5.1 ============================

2026-10-04  Yury Kirsanov
        * [release] 0.4.5 went red in CI and is superseded by this cut;
          nothing of 0.4.5's content changed - see its entry below.

        * [fix] A client whose fleet map was stale was no longer told so:
          the "moved" stamp that lets libperfd re-learn the fleet rode
          only the parked replies of the generic method frame, not those
          of the fixed-layout data verbs the library now uses.  Every
          parked reply carries it.

